---
import SiteLayout from '../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import { FORMAT_REFERENCE, PRIMARY_FORMATS, type FormatReference } from '../content/format-reference';
const formats = PRIMARY_FORMATS
  .map((id) => FORMAT_REFERENCE[id])
  .filter((format): format is FormatReference => Boolean(format));
---
<SiteLayout title="Supported File Formats | ConvertAnyFile" description="Explore supported image, archive, structured-data, and Subtitle Preview formats in ConvertAnyFile, including SRT, VTT, ASS, SSA, SBV, ZIP, 7Z, RAR, CSV, JSON, JPG and more.">
<section class="page-hero"><div class="site-container hero-inner"><div class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span><span>Formats</span></div><span class="eyebrow">Format reference</span><h1>Choose the right format for the job.</h1><p>ConvertAnyFile has three live browser-first categories—Images, Archives, and structured Data—plus a Subtitle Preview. Each category has its own validation, capability rules, safety limits, and certified outputs.</p></div></section>

<section class="content-wide-section"><div class="site-container"><div class="content-intro-grid"><div class="section-heading wide"><span class="section-kicker">Start with the destination</span><h2>Format choice is about the next workflow, not just the extension.</h2><p>A photograph, transparent logo, phone original, archive package, CSV export, API payload, subtitle track, and HDR frame have different requirements. Choose an output based on what the next application accepts and what structure or fidelity must survive the conversion.</p></div><div class="content-summary-card"><strong>How support works</strong><p>Each category has its own validation and output contract. The shared platform does not assume that a file is safe or meaningful just because its filename carries a known extension.</p></div></div></div></section>

<section class="content-wide-section white"><div class="site-container"><div class="section-heading wide"><span class="section-kicker">Subtitle Preview</span><h2>SRT, VTT/WebVTT, ASS, SSA, and SBV timed text.</h2><p>The Preview reads common timed-text formats and writes SRT, VTT, SBV, or plain TXT. ASS and SSA are input-only because their presentation model includes styling, positioning, karaoke, and effects that simpler outputs cannot preserve faithfully.</p></div><div class="comparison-table-wrap"><table class="comparison-table"><thead><tr><th>Format</th><th>Role</th><th>Input</th><th>Output</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>SRT</strong></td><td>Widely compatible numbered subtitle cues</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td><strong>VTT / WebVTT</strong></td><td>Web timed text and browser video captions</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td><strong>ASS</strong></td><td>Styled Advanced SubStation Alpha subtitles</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td><strong>SSA</strong></td><td>Styled SubStation Alpha subtitles</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td><strong>SBV</strong></td><td>Simple timed caption format</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td><strong>TXT</strong></td><td>Readable text extraction</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="related-grid"><a class="related-card" href="/subtitle-converter/">Open Subtitle Preview <span>→</span></a><a class="related-card" href="/srt-to-vtt/">SRT to VTT <span>→</span></a><a class="related-card" href="/vtt-to-srt/">VTT to SRT <span>→</span></a><a class="related-card" href="/ass-to-srt/">ASS to SRT <span>→</span></a></div></div></section>

<section class="content-wide-section"><div class="site-container"><div class="section-heading wide"><span class="section-kicker">Archives</span><h2>ZIP, 7Z, RAR, TAR, TAR.GZ/TGZ, and GZIP.</h2><p>The Archive category can read common archive/container families locally and repack supported regular files and folders. 7Z and supported RAR4/RAR5 are input-only through a lazy-loaded WebAssembly decoder. Certified public outputs are ZIP, TAR, and TAR.GZ.</p></div><div class="comparison-table-wrap"><table class="comparison-table"><thead><tr><th>Format</th><th>Role</th><th>Input</th><th>Output</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>ZIP</strong></td><td>Widely compatible multi-file archive</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td><strong>7Z</strong></td><td>Compressed archive container</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td><strong>RAR</strong></td><td>RAR4/RAR5 archive input</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td><strong>TAR</strong></td><td>Unix-style archive container</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td><strong>TAR.GZ / TGZ</strong></td><td>Gzip-compressed TAR</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td><strong>GZIP / GZ</strong></td><td>Single-file compression stream</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="related-grid"><a class="related-card" href="/archive-converter/">Open Archive Converter <span>→</span></a><a class="related-card" href="/rar-to-zip/">RAR to ZIP <span>→</span></a><a class="related-card" href="/7z-to-zip/">7Z to ZIP <span>→</span></a><a class="related-card" href="/tar-gz-to-zip/">TAR.GZ to ZIP <span>→</span></a></div></div></section>

<section class="content-wide-section white"><div class="site-container"><div class="section-heading wide"><span class="section-kicker">Structured data</span><h2>CSV, TSV, JSON, JSONL, and NDJSON.</h2><p>The Data category accepts five extensions and produces four output families. NDJSON is treated as JSON Lines input because both formats store one complete JSON value per non-empty line.</p></div><div class="comparison-table-wrap"><table class="comparison-table"><thead><tr><th>Format</th><th>Role</th><th>Input</th><th>Output</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>CSV</strong></td><td>Comma-delimited tables</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td><strong>TSV</strong></td><td>Tab-delimited tables</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td><strong>JSON</strong></td><td>Structured objects, arrays, and values</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td><strong>JSONL</strong></td><td>One JSON value per line</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td><strong>NDJSON</strong></td><td>JSON Lines alias/input extension</td><td>Yes</td><td>As JSONL</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="related-grid"><a class="related-card" href="/data-converter/">Open Data Converter <span>→</span></a><a class="related-card" href="/csv-to-json/">CSV to JSON <span>→</span></a><a class="related-card" href="/json-to-csv/">JSON to CSV <span>→</span></a><a class="related-card" href="/jsonl-to-json/">JSONL to JSON <span>→</span></a></div></div></section>

<section class="content-wide-section"><div class="site-container"><div class="section-heading wide"><span class="section-kicker">Certified image outputs</span><h2>{formats.length} image output formats in the production catalog.</h2><p>Every image format below is filtered against the WASM runtime running in the browser. If a deployed build cannot write a codec, the converter hides that option rather than offering a conversion that cannot complete.</p></div><div class="format-grid">{formats.map((format)=><article class="format-card" id={format.id}><span>{format.name}</span><h3>{format.role}</h3><p>{format.summary}</p><p><strong>Best for:</strong> {format.bestFor}</p><p><strong>Trade-off:</strong> {format.tradeoffs}</p></article>)}</div></div></section>

<section class="content-wide-section white"><div class="site-container"><div class="section-heading wide"><span class="section-kicker">Image comparison</span><h2>Compression, transparency, and compatibility.</h2><p>No single column decides the winner. The right image output depends on the content and destination.</p></div><div class="comparison-table-wrap"><table class="comparison-table"><thead><tr><th>Format</th><th>Compression</th><th>Transparency</th><th>Compatibility note</th></tr></thead><tbody>{formats.map((format)=><tr><td><strong>{format.name}</strong></td><td>{format.compression}</td><td>{format.transparency}</td><td>{format.compatibility}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div></div></section>

<section class="content-wide-section"><div class="site-container"><div class="content-intro-grid"><div><span class="section-kicker">Different fidelity contracts</span><h2>Pixels, records, archive trees, and timed text need different rules.</h2><p>Image conversion creates one output image per source and documents flattening behavior for multi-frame or layered inputs. Data conversion uses explicit table/JSON rules. Archive conversion preserves safe regular file trees where representable. Subtitle conversion focuses on cue timing and readable text while making richer presentation loss explicit.</p></div><div class="content-summary-card"><strong>Local first</strong><p>All live categories and the Subtitle Preview use the shared browser-first batch system, while their engines, validation, limits, and output controls remain category-specific.</p></div></div></div></section>

<section class="content-wide-section white"><div class="site-container"><div class="related-grid"><a class="related-card" href="/image-converter/">Open Image Converter <span>→</span></a><a class="related-card" href="/data-converter/">Open Data Converter <span>→</span></a><a class="related-card" href="/archive-converter/">Open Archive Converter <span>→</span></a><a class="related-card" href="/subtitle-converter/">Open Subtitle Preview <span>→</span></a></div></div></section>
</SiteLayout>
